# ${APP_NAME} — autonomous build loop

Runbook for this project's autonomous build loop. Theory, design rationale, and limitations:
[kmp-forge docs/autoloop.md](https://github.com/arthurnagy/kmp-forge/blob/main/docs/autoloop.md).
Installed by `/kmp-forge-add-autoloop` on ${SCAFFOLD_DATE}.

## Launch

```
/loop /kmp-forge-next-increment
```

One invocation = one full increment: take the next `ready` GitHub issue → docs PR → spec gate → code PR → code gate (correctness + conventions + QA) → auto-merge both → close the issue. `/loop` provides the repetition; the loop stops when the queue is empty, when the kill-switch file exists, or on escalation.

## Work queue: GitHub issues

The queue is this repo's open issues labeled **`ready`** — nothing else. Ordered `priority:high` → unlabeled → `priority:low`, then oldest first; `epic` issues are skipped (split them into slice issues). An issue is worked only when its **author and whoever applied `ready`** are the loop's own GitHub account or listed in `ready-approvers` below, and nothing in its **Depends on** section (or GitHub's "blocked by") is still open. Selection is code — `scripts/issues.sh next` in the kmp-forge plugin — not model judgment.

**Only a human applies `ready`.** The loop files follow-up issues (and, with `queue-empty-groom: on`, up to 3 drafted next slices when the queue empties) and moves `in-progress`, but never approves work (the merge guard denies it in `enforce`). An issue filed by someone outside the approvers is raw intake: re-file it as your own (groomed) issue to queue it.

Each increment's change is named `<issue>-<kebab>` (branches `spec/<name>`, `feat/<name>`); the docs PR says `Refs #<issue>`, the code PR `Fixes #<issue>`.

